Almost everything Van Gogh made, he made in the last decade of his life. That is the fact the [museum](https://www.vangoghmuseum.nl/en) is built around: the collection is hung in order across the floors, so you walk out of the dark earth tones of the Dutch years, through Paris, and into the light of the south. Done in sequence it reads as a life rather than a greatest hits, which is the whole argument for coming here rather than seeing four of his paintings elsewhere.

## When to go, and how to walk it

Tickets are timed and sold online only, so this is not a museum you wander into. It opens daily at nine, and on Fridays it stays open into the evening, which is the single best piece of scheduling advice anyone can give you: the late slots are calmer than anything you will find at midday, and the building takes on a different character once the daylight goes out of the atrium.

Otherwise take the first slot of the day. It is busiest between eleven and two, when the tour groups and the day-trippers overlap.

Walk it in order and do not skip the first floor to get to the famous ones. The Sunflowers, the Almond Blossom and the Bedroom draw the crowds and are worth the crowd. The drawings and the letters rarely draw anyone, and they are where the man rather than the brand turns up: give them the extra half hour.

## Where to stop for coffee

- **The museum café**, on the ground floor by the atrium. No ticket needed for the entrance hall side, and a reasonable place to sit before your slot rather than queueing outside it.
- **[Cobra Café](https://www.cobracafe.nl/)**, on the square itself. A terrace facing the lawn, the closest thing to stepping outside without going anywhere.
- **[Brasserie Van Baerle](https://brasserievanbaerle.nl/en/)**, on Van Baerlestraat behind the museum. A long-standing breakfast and lunch room with a garden terrace, and a good deal quieter than anything on the square.
- **[Café Welling](https://www.cafewelling.nl/)**, tucked behind the Concertgebouw on J.W. Brouwersstraat. A neighbourhood cafe that the concert crowd has kept honest for decades.
- **[Café Loetje](https://www.loetje.nl/)**, on Johannes Vermeerstraat. Better known for its steak than its coffee, but a few streets is all it takes to leave the museum quarter behind.

## What to keep an eye out for

The museum runs temporary exhibitions alongside the permanent collection, usually on the same ticket, and they are worth checking before you book rather than discovering on the day. The multimedia guide is genuinely good on the letters, which carry most of the biography the paintings only hint at. And if you are here on a Friday, the evening opening is often paired with music or a talk in the atrium: check the programme, because it changes month to month.

The honest version of the Van Gogh Museum in one visit: book early or book the Friday evening, walk it in order, and give the drawings the half hour nobody else does.
